---
/**
 * The emotional center of the page. Written straight, from the Guild's own
 * Case for Support, in full. No detail here is invented.
 */
import Section from '@/components/ui/Section.astro';
import Picture from '@/components/ui/Picture.astro';
---

<Section tone="stone" labelledby="story-title">
  <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[2fr_3fr] lg:gap-16">
    <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-8 lg:self-start">
      <Picture slot="foundingStory" sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 35vw, 100vw" aspect="4 / 3" />
      <blockquote class="mt-6 max-w-[40ch] font-serif italic leading-relaxed text-ash">
        <p>
          “All these are skilled with their hands, each one an expert at his own work. Without them
          no city could be lived in. Yet they are not sought out for the council of the people… They
          do not sit on the judge’s bench… Nor are they found among the rulers. Yet they maintain
          the fabric of the world…”
        </p>
        <footer class="mt-2 font-sans text-[0.9rem] not-italic">Sirach 38:31–34</footer>
      </blockquote>
    </div>

    <div class="measure flex flex-col gap-5 font-serif text-[1.125rem] leading-[1.7] md:text-[1.2rem]">
      <h2 id="story-title" class="text-h2 font-sans">
        Our Story
      </h2>
      <p>
        It began with one man, one plumbing business, and the Lord as his only companion. A
        working-class tradesman with little more than a budget for small-print classified ads, he
        spent years alone in his van, driving to lonely places and working in the musty basements of
        nearly invisible people. There, the Lord brought him face to face with the working poor: not
        as customers, but as children of God, sitting across from him in their own homes. When it
        came time to settle the bill, he often saw anxiety and embarrassment in their faces. They
        simply could not afford much. So he began doing what he could: a discount here, a free
        service there. Not because it made business sense, but because it was right.
      </p>
      <p>
        There was one client the plumber never forgot: an elderly woman in a wheelchair, living
        alone on a fixed income in a tiny, weathered house tucked away on the backroads of Missouri.
        As he replaced her toilet, she spoke of her loneliness. There seemed to be no family nearby,
        no friends to call, no one to turn to. When the work was finished, she pleaded with him to
        do more. She could not afford other companies. She had no one else to ask. And the question
        lingered: if not him, then who? Who would care for her?
      </p>
      <p>Then the plumber and his poor clients alike found community.</p>
      <p>
        A friend from church, newly established in his own blue-collar business, began partnering
        with him. Then another Catholic tradesman joined. Then another. What began as one man's
        quiet attempt to serve became a shared calling. They prayed before and after each job. They
        traded labor, mentored one another, and showed up when a brother or his family was in need.
        And when it came to serving their clients, they discovered a simple truth: when they asked
        not what they could get, but what they could give, the Lord provided. What started with a
        handful of tradesmen living their faith in the ordinary places of daily work has grown into
        a community, a brotherhood bound by faith, work, and a shared calling to serve.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>
